The loop retries on `TimeoutError` up to `retries` times (default 2, so 3 attempts total). If every attempt times out, the loop exits normally (not via `return` or `throw`), and execution falls through to `return {};` on line 12 — that's the only path that produces the empty object. Any non-timeout error is rethrown immediately via line 9, so it's specifically repeated timeouts that fall through to the empty-object fallback. This looks like an intentional "give up and return a safe default" pattern, but it silently masks the failure from callers — anyone consuming `fetchUser`'s result can't distinguish "user has no data" from "the API never responded," which is worth flagging if callers assume `{}` means a valid empty user.
